well I teach two courses that are LGBT  related introduction to queer cinema is  the first course and in this course it's  basically a study of canonical films  both sort of mainstream films and  experimental films and the periodization  actually starts with at the very  beginning of cinema in 1895 film by  William Dirksen who was working in the  Edison labs with early sort of sound  experimentation Vito Russo who wrote the  classic book on gay cinema called the  celluloid closet calls it the gate  brothers but it's an example of how the  course tries to complicate these films  bringing in contemporary theory and also  locating films within a historical  social political production history so  this film actually is a film that shows  two men dancing while Dirksen himself is  playing the violin and the two men are  actually two sailors on a ship where  there would be no women and the song is  called the song of the cabin boy so  there's a way that it actually captures  a history of same-sex environments  spaces during the late 19th century  early 20th century where men felt like  they could be more intimate in those  spaces and so to call this the gay  brothers doesn't quite capture a kind of  history that they themselves would not  identified as gay probably identified as  heterosexual but in these spaces they  would have felt like it was easier to be  as I say intimate and dancing you know  in sports and and sort of other events  so it's an example of taking sort of  contemporary theory as I say and looking  at a lot of the classical texts but  trying to  problematize them and not allow a kind  of present us reading to basically  monolithically position these films so  the course starts there and runs through  as I say a number of canonical texts to  do this kind of reading that's both  historical and also theoretical some of  the films that we look at our Queen  Christina with Greta Garbo with Alfred  Hitchcock's rope some like it hot  directed by Billy Wilder the boys in the  band directed by William Friedkin desert  hearts which is a lesbian romantic film  independent film in the mid-1980s into  what's called the new queer cinema where  a number of directors Gus Van Sant and  Gregg Araki Tom Kalin were making films  such as my own Private Idaho swoon and  even other films like poison come out of  what's considered to be a new queer  energy that's challenging mainstream  cinema and and trying to come up with  films that are sort of coming from  within gay community and gay  subjectivity instead of industry films  which have been sort of exampled by  Queen Christina rope and boys in the  band and then the course ends with more  contemporary films like Brokeback  Mountain The Kids Are Alright and pariah  a very recent film but mixed in with in  these feature films we put what's being  produced with larger budgets through a  lens of telling a story narrative  against an experimental film which is  often made by queer artists who are  talking about you know their own sort of  response to being gay or lesbian and how  this is either accepted or demonized  within the public sphere  the dominant public sphere so you get  filmmakers like Kenneth egg or Jack  Smith Andy Warhol Barbara Rubin and  others who are making shorter  experimental films that suggests an  alternative cinema to this more  mainstream cinema so this is the films  that we look at and the way that we  approach these films and intro to queer  cinema the other course that I teach is  post-war queer avant-garde cinema which  mainly focuses on experimental film and  it looks at experimental film from 1945  to 1968 and racist the issue that a  number of the classical experimental  films influential films made during this  time period were made by gay men and you  have filmmakers like and I mentioned  those with the intro to queer cinema  course but like Kenneth Anger Jack Smith  Andy Warhol but there are many many  others  Willard Moss Gregory markopolos Curtis  Harrington charles bolton house Jose  Rodriguez Otero and then a number of  actors in the cinema Mario Montez and  Taylor Mead that there's a sense that a  major part of experimental cinema in the  u.s. is coming out of queer community  queer subculture and to think about what  that means that these queer artists  found this space to basically be  creative and to find the means of  production however cheap that was but  also spaces to exhibit this work to be  part of a conversation that in a way is  a conversation that develops that turns  into a conversation that later cinema  will pick up that even Hollywood itself  will end up appropriating after this  period when you look at films like  Midnight Cowboy you know in the late  1960s and other films in the 70s and 80s  they're being influenced by both what's  being produced aesthetically but also  the approach to queer subjectivity in  this earlier cinema so this is a course  that is basically focused on as I'm  calling at this queer avant-garde during  this period it starts in 45 because that  is the end of the war and that's when a  number of these young filmmakers start  to make the films and ends in 1968 and I  used 1968 because this is the moment  when Andy Warhol is shot by by radical  lesbian feminist  who wrote a manifesto called scum and  Jack Smith basically stops making  experimental films and moves into  experimental theater and Kenneth Anger  also is basically at the end of his very  creative realm so to think about why  1968 becomes this kind of key year when  this queer energy shuts down  interestingly a year before Stonewall  and the rise of kind of a new gay and  lesbian energy that arises at this time  period well I also teach in film studies  my position is in film studies American  Studies and LGBT studies so I do teach  courses that are basically a close  analysis of film aesthetics so I teach  the introduction to film studies course  for in for instance and one of the  pleasures I get is you know studying a  film how its put together how meaning is  constructed through every choice that a  filmmaker makes and that can be you know  again costume sets  color you know where the camera is  located you know whether it's a close-up  or an extreme long shot why these  choices are being made and how that  changes the meaning and I basically  bring that into the introduction to  queer cinema and the post-war queer  avant-garde cinema klore course so  within those  courses that I'm teaching for LGBT  studies I'm also teaching a sense of  close analysis and thinking about how  these elements of aesthetics or  narrative construction actually can  construct a queer aesthetic what you can  do differently in the choices that you  make that seem to be speaking from maybe  a sort of queer interior tee or a queer  experience of the world so I think that  actually there's a nice parallel between  what I'm teaching in intro to film with  the queer studies courses  I'm also along with the duties of  programming being on the LGBT studies  committee I chair a committee that is  actually given a small amount of money  to build up the collection of films DVDs  and other media related material for the  film Study Center and and for also the  library at large and so lately because  of the amount of money that I've been  given to do this that this committee has  been given to do this we have found that  we can buy all the DVDs that we want and  there's still money left over so we've  been actually building up the film  collection the 35 millimeter and 16  millimeter film collection at the film  Study Center building up the archive of  LGBT material that can be used in the  classroom and also for film series and  just this year for instance we bought  35-millimeter print of Pedro almodóvar  Matador we were given a 35-millimeter  print of the recent film pariah  with help from an alum Lisa Cortes we've  been building up the 16-millimeter  collection of of the Canon including the  Children's Hour a 1961 film a 1965 film  adaptation of Mary McCarthy's the group  some television dramas really important  from the 1970s again  16-millimeter a question of love with  Gena Rowlands and Jane Alexander a  really important made-for-television  movie which actually is not available on  video so it's a very rare copy what  we've been able to purchase and sixty  millimeter copy of rope and other films  so we are in a really wonderful position  at at Yale and having this support for  again building this material for both  present but also future scholarship for  both undergraduates and graduate  students
 

